<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chapter-riemann-integration" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Riemann Integration
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this chapter, we initiate our study of integral calculus by constructing the Riemann integral and examining its properties. Both because the Riemann integral does not allow for the integration of a wide array of functions and because it does not behave well under limits, we will also begin to prosecute a case for a stronger theory of integration which will be explored further in <xref provisional="part-measure-and-integration"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="./section-riemann-sums-and-integrals/section-riemann-sums-and-integrals.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-the-fundamental-theorems-of-calculus/section-the-fundamental-theorems-of-calculus.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-improper-integration/section-improper-integration.ptx"/>

  <xi:include href="./section-deficiencies-of-the-riemann-integral/section-deficiencies-of-the-riemann-integral.ptx"/>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      In the next chapter, we will explore measure theory, a branch of mathematics invented in order to construct a more robust theory of integration free from the issues mentioned in <xref ref="section-deficiencies-of-the-riemann-integral"/>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
